# Mutation plan for the seven routes of `api4/view.go` (the integrated-boards surface).
#
#   scripts/go-boards.sh start
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/view-routes.plan
#
# **The boards oracle must be running.** Eleven of the twelve parity tests skip without it, and a
# batch run against a suite that skips returns "caught: 0" for reasons that have nothing to do
# with the code. Check the log for twelve tests, not one.
#
# The decisions these cover: the list's `CreateAt` tiebreak (every seeded view shares
# `SortOrder = 0`, so dropping it reorders the answer); `DeleteAt = 0` on the *get* and on the
# *count*, which are separate predicates; the sort rewrite being a move rather than a copy, and
# renumbering from zero under **one** shared `UpdateAt`; the store's out-of-range guard being
# `> len-1` rather than `>= len-1`; the `IsValid` passthrough that keeps a `model.view.is_valid.*`
# id instead of folding it into the app's 500; `createView` overwriting `ChannelId` and `CreatorId`
# from the path and the session; the write gate being `create_post` via membership where the read
# gate has an open-channel fallback; the 201; the trailing newline, which `ReturnStatusOK` does
# not have; and the feature flag itself.
#
# A `\n` in a pattern becomes a newline, so no anchor here can quote Rust's own `b'\n'` — which is
# why `ENCODER_NEWLINE` is a named constant in `views.rs` rather than a literal.
#
# # The tally, and the two survivors that are documented rather than fixed (2026-09-12)
#
# **33 run, 28 caught, both controls SURVIVED** — the first valid run this plan has had. Three
# earlier attempts were void: two had both controls CAUGHT, decided by the two list tests that were
# being served by a stale `SecondServer` on :8082 (see `common::SecondServer::start`), and one
# scored 5 of 33 because nothing started the boards oracle at all.
#
# `store-list-drops-the-createat-tiebreak` was a real gap and is **fixed**: it ran against the `api`
# suite, where the parity fixture's views are created by quick API calls whose `CreateAt` values can
# tie and whose id order can agree with them, so neither answer distinguished the tiebreak. It now
# runs against `crates/mm-store/tests/db_view_store.rs`, whose fixture is built so that ordering by
# `sortorder`, by `createat` and by `id` each give a different answer. CAUGHT there.
#
# Two survivors remain, both **coverage gaps in the parity suite rather than shrugs**, and both are
# recorded here because closing either needs apparatus this plan does not own:
#
# * `api-write-gate-uses-the-read-permission` — swapping `create_post` for `read_channel_content`
#   inside `check_view_write_permission` changes nothing observable, because every caller the suite
#   has either holds both permissions or neither. `session_has_permission_to_channel` reads the
#   membership in both cases, so a non-member of a public channel is refused either way. Catching it
#   needs a member whose role grants `read_channel_content` and **not** `create_post` — a custom
#   channel-scheme role, planted the way `parity_teams_all::user_with_role` plants system roles.
#   Worth doing: this is the gate that separates reading a board from writing one.
# * `api-posts-route-skips-fetching-threads` — flipping `skip_fetch_threads` to `true` is invisible
#   because no fixture view's channel has a **reply**. With only root posts, fetching threads and
#   skipping them return the same list. Catching it needs one threaded reply in the seeded channel.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

